f I discard W2, maybe someone else can use it for a Chow. But I don't have W1 or W3, so it's not part of my potential melds. \n\n- Play W6: Similar to W2. \n\n- Play T8: If I discard T8, maybe someone can use it for a Chow of T7, T8, T9. But I don't have T7 or T9. \n\n- Play B4: I have two B4s. If I discard one, I still have one left. But if I keep both, maybe I can form a Pung if I get another B4. \n\n- Play T6: I have two T6s. Discarding one would leave me with one. \n\n- Play T1: I have two T1s. Discarding one would leave a pair. \n\n- Play W4: I have one W4. \n\n- Play F1: Single tile. \n\nWhich of these is better to discard? \n\nIf I discard B4, I lose the chance to form a Pung. If I discard T6, same. Discarding T1 would break the pair. \n\nSince the Pung of F4 is already there, and I need to form more melds, maybe keeping the B4 pair is better for potential Pung. Similarly with T6. \n\nSo perhaps the best tile to discard is W2 or W6 or W4, which are single tiles not part of any melds. \n\nBetween those, maybe W2 is a lower number tile. If I discard W2, maybe someone can use it for a Chow. But since I don't have the other tiles, it's not a big issue. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W4, which is a middle tile, maybe it's more useful for others. \n\nBut considering that I have a Pung of F4 (North Wind) which gives me 4 points (Seat and Prevalent Wind), and if I can form another Pung or two, that would add more points. \n\nIf I keep the B4 pair, and there are two B4 left in the unshown, maybe I can get one more to form a Pung. That would add another 2 points (Pung of Terminals or Honors, since B4 is a Dot tile, not a terminal. Wait, B4 is a Dot 4. Terminals are 1 and 9. So Pung of B4 would not be a terminal or honor. So that's just a regular Pung. \n\nBut All Pungs is worth 6 points. If I have four Pungs (the F4 and three others), but I don't have enough. \n\nAlternatively, if I can form two Pungs and a pair, that would give me All Pungs (6 points) plus the Pung of F4 (which is part of that). \n\nBut right now, I have one Pung and need two more. \n\nGiven that, maybe keeping the B4 pair and T6 pair is better. \n\nSo, which tile to discard? The W4 is a single tile. If I discard it, I remove a tile that doesn't contribute to any meld. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W2 or W6, same. \n\nBetween these options, maybe discard W4. Because W4 is a middle tile, and if others have Chows, they might need it. But since I don't have W3 or W5, it's not part of my potential. \n\nAlternatively, discard F1. Since it's a single tile, and not part of any meld. \n\nBut F1 is an East Wind. If someone else has a Pung of East Wind, they might need it. But I don't have any. \n\nGiven that, maybe the best move is to discard W4. \n\nWait, but looking at the unshown tiles, W4 has 4 left. So it's more available. Discarding W4 might be safer. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W2, which has 4 left, same. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best choice is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. Since I don't have any Chows or Pungs except the F4, the W2, W4, W6 are all single tiles. \n\nBetween them, maybe discard W4. \n\nSo the answer would be Play W4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a Pung of F4 (North Wind), contributing 4 points (Seat and Prevalent Wind). The remaining tiles include pairs of T1, T6, and B4. To maximize scoring, forming additional melds is key. Discarding W4, a single tile with no immediate meld potential, preserves pairs and allows for future Pungs or Chows in T1, T6, or B4.\n\nReason: Playing W4 removes a non-contributory tile, preserving pairs (T1, T6, B4) that could form Pungs if needed. The Pung of F4 already provides 4 points, and retaining pairs increases the chance of completing All Pungs (6 points) or other high-value patterns like Seven Pairs (24 points) if additional tiles are drawn.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
